1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,000
In Lebanon's Bekaa valley there is a wall with three stones in it.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,566
Each one is about nineteen metres long, and each one weighs roughly as much as two fully loaded jumbo jets.

3
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:15,094
They are called the Trilithon.

4
00:00:15,094 --> 00:00:24,233
Two thousand years ago somebody cut them, moved them, and laid them side by side so neatly you can barely slide a blade between them.

5
00:00:24,699 --> 00:00:26,668
Online the answer is simple.

6
00:00:26,668 --> 00:00:30,746
Nobody in the ancient world could move eight hundred tons.

7
00:00:30,746 --> 00:00:37,566
So the platform must be older than history: a landing pad, built by visitors from somewhere else.

8
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:40,533
So let's treat it like a real job.

9
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:49,723
Imagine we run a heavy-haulage firm, and a client hands us that brief: move the Trilithon, using only what existed back then.

10
00:00:49,723 --> 00:00:50,899
Can we quote it?

11
00:00:51,366 --> 00:00:52,601
Eight line items.

12
00:00:52,601 --> 00:00:54,272
Each one tests a claim.

13
00:00:54,272 --> 00:00:58,340
At the end, we add up the invoice and deliver a verdict.

14
00:00:58,340 --> 00:01:01,899
If the job is impossible, the quote will tell us.

15
00:01:06,366 --> 00:01:08,236
Line one: the site survey.

16
00:01:08,236 --> 00:01:13,917
Baalbek sits near the top of the Bekaa valley, by springs that water the plain.

17
00:01:13,917 --> 00:01:19,166
Archaeologists have found that the hill was lived on since the Neolithic.

18
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:24,806
The Greeks and Romans called the town Heliopolis, the city of the sun.

19
00:01:24,806 --> 00:01:32,417
On top of the old hill the Romans raised a sanctuary to Jupiter, with columns about twenty metres tall.

20
00:01:32,417 --> 00:01:33,599
Six still stand.

21
00:01:34,066 --> 00:01:40,775
Next door is the so-called Temple of Bacchus, one of the best preserved Roman temples anywhere.

22
00:01:40,775 --> 00:01:44,799
UNESCO lists the whole site as a World Heritage property.

23
00:01:45,266 --> 00:01:49,027
Our load sits in the western wall of the temple podium.

24
00:01:49,027 --> 00:01:51,009
Not under the temple, notice.

25
00:01:51,009 --> 00:01:54,633
In the wall that holds the hill up on its steep side.

26
00:01:55,099 --> 00:01:56,492
That detail matters.

27
00:01:56,492 --> 00:02:00,111
The giant blocks appear where the ground falls away.

28
00:02:00,111 --> 00:02:05,122
A retaining wall works better the heavier and more solid its stones are.

29
00:02:05,122 --> 00:02:08,533
Big stones there are engineering, not decoration.

30
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,384
The landing-pad story pictures a flat runway of giant slabs.

31
00:02:13,384 --> 00:02:20,400
Our survey finds something else: a temple platform with the biggest blocks only along one slope.

32
00:02:20,866 --> 00:02:22,320
So the survey is done.

33
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:25,360
We know what the stones do and where they sit.

34
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:28,466
Now we need to know exactly how heavy they are.

35
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:30,336
Line two: the load.

36
00:02:30,336 --> 00:02:38,533
Each Trilithon block is about nineteen metres long, four point two metres high and three point six metres deep.

37
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,957
Let's do the maths ourselves.

38
00:02:40,957 --> 00:02:45,547
That's about two hundred and eighty-seven cubic metres of limestone.

39
00:02:45,547 --> 00:02:52,433
At roughly two point seven tonnes per cubic metre, we get about seven hundred and seventy-five tonnes.

40
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:58,737
That matches the published estimates of seven hundred and fifty to eight hundred tonnes.

41
00:02:58,737 --> 00:03:03,645
The course underneath holds blocks of around three hundred tonnes or more.

42
00:03:03,645 --> 00:03:06,033
Big, but each one is a known number.

43
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:08,530
Now compare the client brief.

44
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:13,569
Erich von Däniken wrote that some blocks weigh nearly two thousand tons.

45
00:03:13,569 --> 00:03:19,799
No surveyed block at Baalbek is that heavy, not even the biggest one still in the quarry.

46
00:03:20,266 --> 00:03:22,186
Weights grow in the retelling.

47
00:03:22,186 --> 00:03:26,410
Eight hundred becomes a thousand, a thousand becomes two thousand.

48
00:03:26,410 --> 00:03:31,466
On our quote we write what the measurements say: about eight hundred per block.

49
00:03:31,933 --> 00:03:33,920
Is eight hundred tonnes a lot?

50
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:34,649
Absolutely.

51
00:03:34,649 --> 00:03:38,756
These are among the heaviest stones ever placed in a building.

52
00:03:38,756 --> 00:03:42,400
That is the honest part of the mystery, and we keep it.

53
00:03:42,866 --> 00:03:45,219
So the load is real, and it is huge.

54
00:03:45,219 --> 00:03:51,233
Next question: where did it come from, and how do you cut something that size out of a hill?

55
00:03:51,699 --> 00:03:53,058
Line three: the cut.

56
00:03:53,058 --> 00:03:54,688
The source is no secret.

57
00:03:54,688 --> 00:04:02,500
About eight hundred to nine hundred metres from the temple lies the ancient quarry, and the stones are still there.

58
00:04:02,966 --> 00:04:10,303
The most famous is the Stone of the Pregnant Woman, roughly twenty metres long and about a thousand tonnes.

59
00:04:10,303 --> 00:04:14,966
It lies tilted, half freed, still joined to the bedrock at its base.

60
00:04:15,433 --> 00:04:17,354
Unfinished blocks are a gift.

61
00:04:17,354 --> 00:04:19,541
They show the method in mid-step.

62
00:04:19,541 --> 00:04:26,166
Narrow channels were cut around the block, and the stone was split along natural layers in the rock.

63
00:04:26,633 --> 00:04:38,533
The German Archaeological Institute's quarry team compares it to how Roman masons cut stone elsewhere: split from the bedrock along the natural fissures, then trimmed to shape.

64
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:49,063
In twenty fourteen that team, with Jeanine Abdul Massih of the Lebanese University, dug around the Pregnant Woman to study the cutting and transport.

65
00:04:49,063 --> 00:04:54,466
They documented tool marks and searched the old spoil heaps for datable pottery.

66
00:04:54,933 --> 00:04:56,591
The brief says lasers.

67
00:04:56,591 --> 00:05:01,037
But a laser leaves smooth melted faces, not chisel grooves.

68
00:05:01,037 --> 00:05:05,333
What the quarry shows is iron tools, wedges and patience.

69
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:11,800
So we can quote the cut: a quarry crew, iron tools, and months of work per block.

70
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:13,429
Hard, slow, expensive.

71
00:05:13,429 --> 00:05:14,540
Not mysterious.

72
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:17,133
The real question is the next line.

73
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:19,040
Line four: the route.

74
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:20,893
Any hauler asks this first.

75
00:05:20,893 --> 00:05:21,785
Is it uphill?

76
00:05:21,785 --> 00:05:28,166
Here is the good news for the Romans: the quarry is slightly higher than the temple platform.

77
00:05:28,633 --> 00:05:42,266
Researchers who studied the ground, from Jean-Pierre Adam in the nineteen seventies onward, point out that a block could travel from quarry to wall on a level or gently falling road, without ever being lifted.

78
00:05:42,733 --> 00:05:44,544
That changes the whole job.

79
00:05:44,544 --> 00:05:48,435
You don't need to raise eight hundred tonnes into the sky.

80
00:05:48,435 --> 00:05:53,399
You need to drag it across less than a kilometre of prepared, level track.

81
00:05:53,866 --> 00:05:59,294
Adam also noticed that the unfinished blocks sit raised and tilted in their pits.

82
00:05:59,294 --> 00:06:07,066
That leaves room to slide timber underneath as the block is freed, so it can end up on rollers without being lifted.

83
00:06:07,533 --> 00:06:12,794
The brief imagines anti-gravity because it assumes the stones had to fly.

84
00:06:12,794 --> 00:06:15,533
The route says they only had to slide.

85
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:17,958
Distance, under a kilometre.

86
00:06:17,958 --> 00:06:19,916
Gradient, slightly downhill.

87
00:06:19,916 --> 00:06:23,133
Now the expensive line: the crew and the gear.

88
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:25,366
Line five: crew and gear.

89
00:06:25,366 --> 00:06:29,818
The key machine is the capstan: a vertical drum with long bars.

90
00:06:29,818 --> 00:06:35,966
People walk in a circle, and the drum winds a rope with many times their pulling force.

91
00:06:36,433 --> 00:06:42,481
Chain several capstans together, anchor them in the ground ahead of the block, and pull.

92
00:06:42,481 --> 00:06:50,866
Margarete van Ess of the German institute named the capstan as the most likely method, with sledges also under discussion.

93
00:06:51,333 --> 00:06:52,409
How many people?

94
00:06:52,409 --> 00:06:56,848
An old estimate claimed forty thousand men for the Pregnant Woman.

95
00:06:56,848 --> 00:07:06,399
Jean-Pierre Adam worked through the mechanics and arrived at a far smaller crew: on the order of one hundred and forty-four, working capstans.

96
00:07:06,866 --> 00:07:07,907
Too optimistic?

97
00:07:07,907 --> 00:07:09,295
We have a test case.

98
00:07:09,295 --> 00:07:21,233
In seventeen sixty-nine and seventy, the Russians moved the Thunder Stone, a granite boulder of well over a thousand tonnes, to become the base of Peter the Great's statue.

99
00:07:21,699 --> 00:07:24,232
They used no animals and no engines.

100
00:07:24,232 --> 00:07:35,699
Around four hundred men dragged it on a sledge riding over bronze balls in grooved tracks, about six kilometres overland, roughly two hundred metres on a good day.

101
00:07:36,166 --> 00:07:41,344
That stone was heavier than any Trilithon block, and its route was longer.

102
00:07:41,344 --> 00:07:45,333
The job was done with human muscle in the age of candles.

103
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:56,522
And the Romans had their own heavy kit: treadwheel cranes, compound pulleys and capstans, shown on monuments and described by engineers like Vitruvius.

104
00:07:56,522 --> 00:08:01,066
They lifted column parts weighing many tonnes at Baalbek itself.

105
00:08:01,533 --> 00:08:06,988
So line five comes out at hundreds of workers, not tens of thousands.

106
00:08:06,988 --> 00:08:09,439
Expensive, organised, imperial.

107
00:08:09,439 --> 00:08:11,099
No visitors required.

108
00:08:11,566 --> 00:08:13,370
Line six: the placement.

109
00:08:13,370 --> 00:08:16,375
Here is a clue on the stones themselves.

110
00:08:16,375 --> 00:08:20,358
Many Roman blocks carry lifting holes for iron grips.

111
00:08:20,358 --> 00:08:24,866
The Trilithon blocks show no visible lifting sockets on top.

112
00:08:25,333 --> 00:08:26,566
That fits the route.

113
00:08:26,566 --> 00:08:36,866
If the lower courses were built first and earth was banked up behind and in front of them, the big block could be slid along at ground level, straight onto its course.

114
00:08:37,333 --> 00:08:44,344
Then the final centimetres: levers, wedges and patience, nudging it into place until the joint closes.

115
00:08:44,344 --> 00:08:50,600
Afterwards the temporary earth comes away, and the stone appears to float above the ground.

116
00:08:51,066 --> 00:08:53,565
The tight joints impress everyone.

117
00:08:53,565 --> 00:08:57,829
But the same precision is found across Roman architecture.

118
00:08:57,829 --> 00:09:02,533
Fine joints are a sign of skilled masons, not of alien machines.

119
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,574
The brief says the blocks were levitated into place.

120
00:09:06,574 --> 00:09:09,392
The stones themselves say they were slid.

121
00:09:09,392 --> 00:09:14,066
No lifting holes, a level road, and a wall built from the ground up.

122
00:09:14,533 --> 00:09:17,480
So the haul and the placement are quotable.

123
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:18,714
Now the paperwork.

124
00:09:18,714 --> 00:09:21,866
When was this job actually done, and for whom?

125
00:09:22,333 --> 00:09:24,217
Line seven: the paperwork.

126
00:09:24,217 --> 00:09:26,029
Every job leaves records.

127
00:09:26,029 --> 00:09:30,522
The brief says the platform is tens of thousands of years old.

128
00:09:30,522 --> 00:09:31,900
The site disagrees.

129
00:09:32,366 --> 00:09:39,677
First clue: researchers reported part of a Roman column drum under the course that carries the Trilithon.

130
00:09:39,677 --> 00:09:44,133
Whatever lies under a stone was there before the stone was laid.

131
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:52,250
Second clue: a Roman architect's sketch of the temple's gable, engraved on top of one of the Trilithon blocks.

132
00:09:52,250 --> 00:09:56,006
Some scholars say a sketch alone can't date the stone.

133
00:09:56,006 --> 00:09:59,066
Taken with the rest, it points the same way.

134
00:09:59,533 --> 00:10:10,600
Third: a graffito on one of the top column drums shows the Temple of Jupiter was standing almost to its capitals by around AD sixty, in the reign of Nero.

135
00:10:11,066 --> 00:10:12,973
And there is an older layer.

136
00:10:12,973 --> 00:10:19,510
German researchers found that a large pre-Roman terrace stood here first and was never finished.

137
00:10:19,510 --> 00:10:23,799
The Roman podium was wrapped around it in the first century AD.

138
00:10:24,266 --> 00:10:37,233
Margarete van Ess sums it up: the Temple of Jupiter was definitely built by the Romans, in at least four phases, from around fifteen BC into the early third century AD.

139
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:40,481
So where did the landing pad come from?

140
00:10:40,481 --> 00:10:47,757
In nineteen fifty-nine a Soviet mathematician, Matest Agrest, suggested the terrace was a launch site.

141
00:10:47,757 --> 00:10:54,533
Zecharia Sitchin later claimed the Epic of Gilgamesh describes rockets at a Landing Place here.

142
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:02,382
But researchers who checked point out that the Epic of Gilgamesh contains no rocket launch and no Landing Place.

143
00:11:02,382 --> 00:11:06,666
The paperwork for the alien theory is from the twentieth century.

144
00:11:07,133 --> 00:11:09,359
Line eight: the abandoned loads.

145
00:11:09,359 --> 00:11:12,907
Every big job has a pile of things that went wrong.

146
00:11:12,907 --> 00:11:16,733
At Baalbek, they are the most impressive stones of all.

147
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:21,116
The German team found why the Pregnant Woman stayed put.

148
00:11:21,116 --> 00:11:23,844
At one edge the stone quality was poor.

149
00:11:23,844 --> 00:11:26,222
It could have broken in transport.

150
00:11:26,222 --> 00:11:29,299
So they left a thousand tonnes where it lay.

151
00:11:29,766 --> 00:11:33,758
And right beside it, in twenty fourteen, they found another.

152
00:11:33,758 --> 00:11:45,866
About nineteen point six metres long, six wide, at least five and a half high: roughly one thousand six hundred and fifty tonnes, the largest worked stone block known from antiquity.

153
00:11:46,333 --> 00:11:49,922
Local legends filled the gap long before aliens did.

154
00:11:49,922 --> 00:11:51,786
Giants built it for Nimrod.

155
00:11:51,786 --> 00:11:59,033
Solomon built it with spirits, and the blocks were left in the quarry because the spirits went on strike.

156
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:09,011
The stone's own name comes from a story: a pregnant woman promised to reveal how to move it, if the townspeople fed her until she gave birth.

157
00:12:09,011 --> 00:12:10,833
She never revealed a thing.

158
00:12:11,299 --> 00:12:13,930
The true open question is why so big.

159
00:12:13,930 --> 00:12:18,408
Van Ess says the podium stones are bigger than they need to be.

160
00:12:18,408 --> 00:12:25,233
Her institute studies exactly that: projects built to impress, at the edge of what was possible.

161
00:12:25,700 --> 00:12:26,910
The final invoice.

162
00:12:26,910 --> 00:12:29,399
Let's add up every line of the quote.

163
00:12:29,866 --> 00:12:34,502
A quarry under a kilometre away, with matching blocks: evidence.

164
00:12:34,502 --> 00:12:37,472
A route that never goes uphill: evidence.

165
00:12:37,472 --> 00:12:42,904
Roman dating from the drum, the sketch and the AD sixty graffito: evidence.

166
00:12:42,904 --> 00:12:47,033
Capstans and rollers with hundreds of workers: plausible.

167
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:50,187
An alien landing pad: no evidence.

168
00:12:50,187 --> 00:12:54,850
Two thousand tons and tens of thousands of years: inflated.

169
00:12:54,850 --> 00:13:00,700
The exact haulage method, and why the stones are so oversized: still open.

170
00:13:01,166 --> 00:13:02,004
The verdict.

171
00:13:02,004 --> 00:13:14,299
The Trilithon was moved by Roman builders in the first century AD, from a quarry just up the road, on a route that never climbed, with capstans, rollers and a great many hands.

172
00:13:14,766 --> 00:13:19,446
The job was real, and it was close to the limit of the ancient world.

173
00:13:19,446 --> 00:13:20,600
Just not past it.

174
00:13:20,600 --> 00:13:26,433
In seventeen seventy another crew moved an even heavier stone with nothing but muscle.

175
00:13:26,899 --> 00:13:32,592
What stays open is human: why one city wanted stones this big, and why they stopped.

176
00:13:32,592 --> 00:13:37,200
The quarry still holds the answer, under a few more metres of earth.

177
00:13:37,666 --> 00:13:41,745
If you ran the haulage firm, what would you charge for this job?

178
00:13:41,745 --> 00:13:47,100
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
